### Summary
When debug messaging is enabled using `hb_buffer_set_message_func`, a maliciously crafted font can trigger a buffer overflow using a complicated sequence lookup, leading to unauthorised overwriting of other data.

### Details
In an OpenType font with a GSUB table, sequence lookups (or more formally, Multiple Substitution lookups) can be used to replace one glyph with many. A single application of a sequence lookup can replace a glyph with up to 2^16 glyphs, provided the maximum length of the font buffer is large enough. If a sequence lookup adds a large number of glyphs in this way, and debug messaging is enabled, a buffer overflow will be triggered.

The exact cause of the overflow is a section in Sequence.hh, which I will reproduce here:

```
 if (HB_BUFFER_MESSAGE_MORE && c->buffer->messaging ())
    {
      c->buffer->sync_so_far ();

      char buf[HB_MAX_CONTEXT_LENGTH * 16] = {0};
      char *p = buf;

      for (unsigned i = c->buffer->idx - count; i < c->buffer->idx; i++)
      {
	if (buf < p)
	  *p++ = ',';
	snprintf (p, sizeof(buf) - (p - buf), "%u", i);
	p += strlen(p);
      }

      c->buffer->message (c->font,
			  "multiplied glyphs at %s",
			  buf);
    }
```
This section is ran immediately after a sequence lookup has been run. If `c->buffer->messaging ()` is enabled, ie if debug messaging is in use, the code will allocate a buffer of `HB_MAX_CONTEXT_LENGTH * 16` characters. By default `HB_MAX_CONTEXT_LENGTH` is set to 64, so the buffer will be 1024 characters. The code will then attempt to write into this buffer a list of the indices of the newly added glyphs, and then give the list to the messaging function. Unfortunately if too many glyphs were added, this list will overfill the buffer. The exact number of glyphs needed varies based on where they are placed (since the indices could have a varying number of digits), but if the glyphs were placed at indices of 0,1,2,3 and so on, the buffer will be overfilled if there are 296 glyphs or more added.

There are two potential places where the buffer could be overflowed. The first one is with the writing of the ',' between indices, which has no bounds check whatsoever. The second one is in the `snprintf` function used to write the indices, which appears to have a bounds check, but the check doesn't actually work properly. This is because the bounds check does not prevent the pointer `p` being incremented, which means that `p` could exceed the end of the buffer; if this happens, in subsequent calls to `snprintf` there will be an integer underflow in the bounds check (which is an unsigned integer), and so the bound will be set to a very large and useless value.

### PoC
To activate this issue, create a font that has a sequence lookup that injects at least 296 glyphs in one go. Create an application that runs harfbuzz and sets a message function (it doesn't hugely matter what the message function does, it just has to be set). Then in that application, load the font and trigger the sequence lookup.

### Impact
Most font applications do not use debug messaging. The main threat here is to websites that use shaper debugging - if something like [crowbar](https://github.com/simoncozens/crowbar) were created that ran server-side instead of client-side, it would be vulnerable. However, I have not been able to find any vulnerable sites that currently exist.
